  "notes": "SENSE 1 (regret):\nOften used in the ている form to express ongoing regret. The set phrase {悔|く}やんでも{悔|く}やみきれない means \"no amount of regret is enough\" and expresses deep, inconsolable regret.\n\nSENSE 2 (condolences):\nThe noun form {悔|く}やみ means \"condolence.\" The formal expression お{悔|く}やみ{申|もう}し{上|あ}げます is the standard way to offer condolences at funerals.\n\nCOMMON COLLOCATIONS:\n- {悔|く}やんでも{悔|く}やみきれない (inconsolable regret)\n- お{悔|く}やみの{言葉|ことば} (words of condolence)\n- {過去|かこ}を{悔|く}やむ (to regret the past)\n\nRelated: {後悔|こうかい}する is the more common word for \"to regret\" in everyday speech. {悔|く}やむ has a more literary or emotional tone.",
